```

That single equation anchors the definition of representation.

A convention statement for generators. The draft alternates between anti-Hermitian generators such as $-d/dx$ and Hermitian generators such as

```math
K=-i\partial_x.
```

Choose one convention or explain the conversion once. The same issue affects whether

```math
[L_x,L_y]=L_z
```

or

```math
[L_x,L_y]=iL_z.
```

One paragraph extending one-dimensional translation to commuting multidimensional translations:

```math
e^{ik_xx}e^{ik_yy}e^{ik_zz}
=
e^{i\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf x}.
```

A sentence naming the Weyl–Heisenberg group behind the CCR. That helps prevent it from sounding like an unnamed rival to spacetime symmetry.

A final boundary statement: Symmetry determines the allowable form and conserved structure of a theory; it does not alone choose every dynamical relation.

That sentence would prevent several later overclaims.

**My strongest recommendation**

Move the general commutator, Casimir, and conservation material directly after continuous generators. Then let translation begin a long uninterrupted climb:

```math
\text{translation}
\rightarrow
\text{functions}
\rightarrow
\text{unitarity}
\rightarrow
\text{plane waves}
\rightarrow
\text{Fourier decomposition}
\rightarrow
\text{canonical commutator}
\rightarrow
